# Arithmetic Lefschetz–Stepanov theory

Stepanov's half of Weil's proof over `Spec ℤ`: which Frobenius, which auxiliaries, which orders of
vanishing and which identity plays the role of `x^q = x`. The exact Frobenius of the integer
auxiliaries is the Adams operation, which is the Galois action on the torsion of the prime torus and
the dilation on the prime orbit; the norm is its integrality amplifier. Exact vanishing on the orbit
is confined to countably many heights; exact order at any point costs the degree; the closed points
of the prime circles are counted at Chebyshev's price. The Lefschetz numbers of the Frobenius
graphs of degree `≤ N` are the degree of the minimal auxiliary vanishing at their fixed points, so
RH is an upper bound on that degree; the bound fails for the integer-norm one-off. Siegel
auxiliaries and a zero estimate uniform in the number of logarithms are the tools. Polynomial
syzygies have image exactly the powers of the full affine Adams fixed-point ideal; integral
Newton coordinates give their sharp arithmetic length and all minimal auxiliaries. Every rational
self-map defined on and preserving a finite prime orbit with two distinct primes is a translated
Laurent Adams map, without an assumed regularity of its clock. Polynomial maps have nonnegative
exponents; rational maps allow every integer exponent. Real or integer coefficients remove the
translation. A nonidentity rational orbit preserver cannot fix two orbit points.
The same rational classification holds on the full prime algebra, even when successive
coordinates introduce new primes. Six exponentials and Baker independence remove the
finite invariant prime-set restriction.
See `ArithmeticLefschetzStepanovTheory/README.md`.
-/
